  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM CDT Wed May 13 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nOur break in howling winds occurring today with upper ridging\nmoving into the Plains and surface high from the eastern Plains\ninto the Upper Midwest. A strong shortwave trough will begin to\ndig into Saskatchewan and the northern Rockies tonight, with\nsouth to southeasterly winds picking up ahead of the trough. The\nlow level jet starts ramping up late tonight, and some of the\nmodels start mixing with momentum transfer starting early\nThursday morning. Winds slow down a bit as the upper low moves\ninto Manitoba and the surface trough axis arrives in the Red\nRiver Valley, but winds pick up again out of the west as 980mb\nlow tracks across southern Canada. Cold front starts to push\ndown into the region late Friday into Saturday. Flow briefly\nbecomes southwesterly Sunday into Monday ahead of the next\ntrough for mid-week. Several of the model runs bring better\nmoisture up into the region and ECMWF EFI has a slight wet\nsignal starting to nose up into southeastern ND during that\nperiod. \n\n...Critical Fire Weather Thursday and Friday...\n\nRH values this afternoon have fallen into the 20s and even some\nteens in eastern ND. Fortunately the winds are light and\nvariable to keep fire weather below critical. That will change\nhowever as the surface low tracking through southern Canada\nbrings strong southeast then westerly winds tomorrow into\nFriday. RH values will not be as low thanks to some clouds and\nthe possibility of a few high-based showers on Thursday, mainly\nfrom the Red River Valley eastward. However, there is still a \n50-60 percent chance for RH to drop below 25 percent in that\narea for a few hours. RH values will be even lower west of the\nRRV. Put out a Red Flag warning for all but the far eastern tier\nof counties. A bit of a break in wind late Thursday into Friday\nas the trough axis moves through, but then winds pick up again\nout of the west. While the 850mb winds not quite as strong as\nThursday, Friday will see some strong mixing with pressure rises\nand some cold air advection starting to come down. RH values\nwill drop sharply, down close to 20 percent or even lower by\nafternoon. Continued with the fire weather watch for all of our\nCWA.\n\n...Winds Thursday and Friday...\n\nNBM tries to push the winds both ahead of the trough on Thursday\nand on Friday up to 35 to 40 kts sustained. While this seems\nexcessive since it is more 90th percentile instead of middle of\nthe ensemble, there is high probability of gusts over 40 mph.\nNot confident enough to go high wind warning for our counties,\nbut advisory seems likely. Kept the advisory just for Thursday \nfor now but will likely need another wind headline of some sort \nfor Friday as probability of gusts above 50 mph is 60 to 70\npercent in some areas.",
